1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,240
You're looking at the dominant entertainment medium of the day,

2
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:08,500
complete with screeching high-speed chases and explosive gunplay.

3
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:10,920
But no, it's not a movie.

4
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:14,600
It's a game, an interactive video game.

5
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:18,380
You go through the same process you would in making a great film,

6
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:20,600
but then you apply the technology.

7
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:25,000
What you must do with the technology is make it simple, hot, and deep.

8
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:30,000
Games have been with us since the beginning of time,

9
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:33,500
but these are the first games to use computer technology.

10
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:35,340
In fact, they don't just use it.

11
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:37,440
They drive it.

12
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:38,940
If it weren't for video games,

13
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:41,320
you wouldn't have the computer or Internet revolution

14
00:00:41,420 --> 00:00:42,520
that you have today.

15
00:00:42,620 --> 00:00:45,580
If it weren't for video games humanizing computers,

16
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:47,660
you wouldn't see them in everybody's home.

17
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:50,100
Unlike any games before in history,

18
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:53,600
these games play with you one-on-one in their world,

19
00:00:53,601 --> 00:00:55,360
in their world, not yours.

20
00:00:55,460 --> 00:00:58,900
A virtual world of 3D graphics and stereo sound

21
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,740
capable of inspiring an emotional, physical,

22
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:04,240
and even psychological experience.

23
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:07,840
A video game is, I guess it's a joyride to insanity.

24
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:13,260
It's a journey to our inner nightmares and fantasies.

25
00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:19,200
But they weren't always so sophisticated.

26
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:21,400
Once, they were nothing more than a beam of light

27
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:22,900
on a cathode ray tube.

28
00:01:22,901 --> 00:01:26,600
How they evolved into high-tech computer art and science

29
00:01:26,601 --> 00:01:29,300
is the story of humankind's urge to play

30
00:01:29,301 --> 00:01:32,760
and of the growth of computers into today's dominant medium.

31
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:37,700
What began as a play-inspired tinkering at an MIT computer

32
00:01:37,701 --> 00:01:39,820
has turned into a billion-dollar industry,

33
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:42,260
offering thousands of software titles

34
00:01:42,261 --> 00:01:44,120
and a variety of hardware platforms

35
00:01:44,121 --> 00:01:45,700
for the amusement of millions.

36
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:48,320
And it all happened just for the fun of it.

37
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:12,900
Music

38
00:02:12,901 --> 00:02:15,220
In living rooms across the United States,

39
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:17,460
the youth of America and the young at heart

40
00:02:17,461 --> 00:02:19,780
are playing games, video games.

41
00:02:19,781 --> 00:02:23,480
Home computers and television sets fed by game consoles

42
00:02:23,580 --> 00:02:28,020
have become the virtual playground for a new generation.

43
00:02:28,120 --> 00:02:31,920
They're full of action and adventure,

44
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:37,840
battles between good and evil, quests and cyber shootouts,

45
00:02:37,940 --> 00:02:41,640
and all kinds of races and sports contests.

46
00:02:41,740 --> 00:02:44,240
For many, this is a world so much bigger in scope

47
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:46,380
and richer in possibilities that there's

48
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:47,840
no better place to play.

49
00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:48,840
What did I get?

50
00:02:48,841 --> 00:02:51,840
The game space becomes a virtual play space.

51
00:02:51,940 --> 00:02:53,140
It becomes an environment.

52
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:55,480
They can do the things that boys used to do

53
00:02:55,580 --> 00:02:57,480
in their imagination, sitting in their backyard

54
00:02:57,580 --> 00:02:58,980
in suburban America.

55
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:00,180
They can take risks.

56
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:01,380
They can take challenges.

57
00:03:01,480 --> 00:03:02,880
They can compete with each other.

58
00:03:02,980 --> 00:03:06,500
They can have larger than life, blood and thunder adventure

59
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:08,600
that really gets the blood pumping.

60
00:03:13,640 --> 00:03:16,440
Video games are the virtual place to hang out,

61
00:03:16,441 --> 00:03:20,440
a kind of electronic alleyway where boys will be boys

62
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:23,080
and fantasies will come to virtual life.

63
00:03:23,180 --> 00:03:25,680
Are you in love with her?

64
00:03:25,780 --> 00:03:27,180
Love.

65
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:29,080
Love is for the living, Sal.

66
00:03:29,180 --> 00:03:31,080
I'm only after her for one reason.

67
00:03:31,180 --> 00:03:32,680
She's my ticket out of here.

68
00:03:32,780 --> 00:03:34,200
You are there.

69
00:03:34,300 --> 00:03:35,400
You are inside.

70
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:36,800
You are making decisions.

71
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:38,800
You are seeing the results of what you are doing.

72
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:43,300
You're having a fantasy and a spectacular experience.

73
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:45,600
For all their fantasy, video games

74
00:03:45,601 --> 00:03:49,300
Video games duplicate reality in exacting detail.

75
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:51,300
Just how they do that is a marvel

76
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:53,000
of several different technologies

77
00:03:53,100 --> 00:03:55,040
that have emerged since the birth of video games

78
00:03:55,140 --> 00:03:56,640
in the early 60s.

79
00:03:56,740 --> 00:03:58,240
One of the most essential is something

80
00:03:58,340 --> 00:03:59,840
called texture mapping.

81
00:03:59,940 --> 00:04:02,140
It turns the game into a three-dimensional,

82
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:05,960
photorealistic, immersive experience.

83
00:04:06,060 --> 00:04:09,760
In the 1980s, games, there was no texture mapping.

84
00:04:09,860 --> 00:04:13,660
And when you created an object, if you had a spacecraft,

85
00:04:13,760 --> 00:04:15,200
it would just be plain.

86
00:04:15,201 --> 00:04:18,940
there wouldn't be any detail on the surfaces.

87
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:22,940
We went to some of the MIT, one of the MIT tech libraries,

88
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:25,680
and saw that on high-end supercomputers and graphic

89
00:04:25,780 --> 00:04:28,340
workstations, they were doing texture mapping.

90
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:31,620
And we did some trials to see whether we could do that

91
00:04:31,720 --> 00:04:33,360
on a PC at the time.

92
00:04:33,460 --> 00:04:35,160
This is in 1990.

93
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:36,960
Found out, yes, it would actually work.

94
00:04:37,060 --> 00:04:39,060
When the Trade Federation Viceroy arrives,

95
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:41,500
we must convince him to leave the system.

96
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:44,700
Be prepared for the unexpected.

97
00:04:44,701 --> 00:04:47,600
The room is filling with poison gas.

98
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:51,000
Texture mapping adds photo realism to a game.

99
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:55,140
It's eye-catching and beautiful.

100
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:57,740
It offers the look and feel of real worlds.

101
00:05:02,120 --> 00:05:04,880
It grew out of the development of more powerful computer

102
00:05:04,980 --> 00:05:07,560
microprocessors and advanced games

103
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:10,100
beyond monochrome images.

104
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:12,860
You can take a texture, and instead of putting it

105
00:05:12,861 --> 00:05:21,860
it on the cube, you're putting it on a more rounded sphere that maybe has a nose and some

106
00:05:21,861 --> 00:05:28,160
other details of the shape, and then the textures can be much more detailed.

107
00:05:28,161 --> 00:05:32,940
And all of a sudden you have this very real looking character or real looking desk or

108
00:05:32,941 --> 00:05:38,020
real looking chandelier, and it's really incredible.

109
00:05:38,021 --> 00:05:43,700
And then once it's in the engine and it has all its motions put on it and it has its sound,

110
00:05:43,701 --> 00:05:44,700
it's really very immersive.

111
00:05:44,701 --> 00:05:49,740
All of a sudden you're walking and talking to this person that, you know, was just a

112
00:05:49,741 --> 00:05:50,740
cube.

113
00:05:50,741 --> 00:05:57,620
A computer simulated game takes an average of 24 months of full-time work from a staff

114
00:05:57,621 --> 00:05:58,620
of 20.

115
00:05:58,621 --> 00:06:03,980
There are thousands of details, and at times things go wrong, as they did in the texture

116
00:06:03,981 --> 00:06:07,980
mapping of a torpedo attack sequence for a combat simulation game.

117
00:06:07,981 --> 00:06:10,980
The torpedoes are dropping, they're hitting the water, right?

118
00:06:10,981 --> 00:06:12,980
And they're making splashes, right?

119
00:06:12,981 --> 00:06:16,980
Yet, uh, when they hit an object, uh, they pass right through.

120
00:06:16,981 --> 00:06:18,980
Okay? We need that fixed right away.

121
00:06:18,981 --> 00:06:21,980
Well, it turns out that our boats, right, for rendering purposes

122
00:06:21,981 --> 00:06:24,980
and for cheating purposes, uh, we don't render the keels

123
00:06:24,981 --> 00:06:25,980
under the water.

124
00:06:25,981 --> 00:06:28,980
So what's happening is the torpedo, which is traveling at 15 feet

125
00:06:28,981 --> 00:06:30,980
under the water, as it should in real life,

126
00:06:30,981 --> 00:06:32,980
in real life, because we're simulating a torpedo,

127
00:06:32,981 --> 00:06:34,220
is missing the boat.

128
00:06:34,221 --> 00:06:36,280
From a water perspective, looking at the water,

129
00:06:36,281 --> 00:06:38,220
you wouldn't see underneath the boat.

130
00:06:38,221 --> 00:06:40,880
So now it's, oh god, we have to go back and redo the model.

131
00:06:40,881 --> 00:06:45,560
We have to add an underneath to the model

132
00:06:45,561 --> 00:06:49,360
so that the torpedo knows to hit the boat and to blow it up.

133
00:06:49,361 --> 00:06:52,500
When it's done, the game's environments are so real,

134
00:06:52,501 --> 00:06:54,740
there's no need to suspend disbelief.

135
00:06:54,741 --> 00:06:56,980
It looks like the real thing.

136
00:06:56,981 --> 00:06:58,860
But not only have the game environments

137
00:06:58,861 --> 00:07:00,760
reached a level of reality, so have

138
00:07:00,761 --> 00:07:03,520
of game characters because of another technology

139
00:07:03,620 --> 00:07:05,400
called motion capture.

140
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:07,400
It's the ultimate realization of a technique

141
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:12,200
used by Walt Disney to help animators draw Snow White.

142
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:14,200
Motion capture reached a new height

143
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:16,300
with video games in the 90s.

144
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:19,440
Motion capture electronically copies a movement,

145
00:07:19,540 --> 00:07:22,080
like Tiger Woods driving a golf ball,

146
00:07:22,180 --> 00:07:24,760
and turns it into a computer image.

147
00:07:24,860 --> 00:07:28,360
Wired from head to toe, every movement of a desired action,

148
00:07:28,361 --> 00:07:30,360
every turn and twist of a body,

149
00:07:30,361 --> 00:07:34,360
every arm and leg in motion is accurately copied.

150
00:07:34,361 --> 00:07:37,360
An earlier generation of games relied on an artist's

151
00:07:37,361 --> 00:07:40,360
interpretation and rendition of the body's movement.

152
00:07:40,361 --> 00:07:42,360
But motion capture makes every movement real,

153
00:07:42,361 --> 00:07:48,360
from a sword stroke to a slap shot or a slam dunk.

154
00:07:48,361 --> 00:07:51,360
He makes the cast.

155
00:07:51,361 --> 00:07:54,360
Added to image is the dimension of sound.

156
00:07:54,361 --> 00:07:57,360
The reality created in the games of today

157
00:07:57,361 --> 00:07:59,760
is done with voices, music, and sound effects

158
00:07:59,860 --> 00:08:01,760
that rival the quality of a Hollywood movie.

159
00:08:01,860 --> 00:08:04,060
Oh, my!

160
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:07,700
Sound studios and star voices punctuate the games.

161
00:08:07,800 --> 00:08:09,600
It'll be a hell of a lot easier to get to crash sites.

162
00:08:14,120 --> 00:08:16,520
If today's games look, feel, and sound

163
00:08:16,620 --> 00:08:19,720
like major motion pictures, the very first computer game

164
00:08:19,820 --> 00:08:22,560
resembled a low-budget sci-fi movie.

165
00:08:22,660 --> 00:08:24,520
The game was called Space War.

166
00:08:24,521 --> 00:08:28,520
It was developed in 1962 at MIT by a graduate student

167
00:08:28,521 --> 00:08:30,020
named Steve Russell.

168
00:08:30,021 --> 00:08:33,520
Space War had only two factors in common with today's games.

169
00:08:33,521 --> 00:08:35,020
First, it shot something.

170
00:08:35,021 --> 00:08:37,720
And second, it was played on a computer.

171
00:08:37,721 --> 00:08:39,120
But what a computer.

172
00:08:39,121 --> 00:08:42,620
It was an $8 million mainframe that was used to calculate

173
00:08:42,621 --> 00:08:44,020
missile trajectories.

174
00:08:44,021 --> 00:08:48,520
It had no keyboard, no mouse, no CD-ROM, and no audio system.

175
00:08:48,521 --> 00:08:52,420
Computers were big blocks of vacuum tubes and scary things

176
00:08:52,421 --> 00:08:56,720
where you had to communicate via punch cards or tape with hole in it

177
00:08:56,721 --> 00:09:00,260
or basically some kind of huge magnetic spool of tape.

178
00:09:00,261 --> 00:09:03,840
Now, all of a sudden, for the first time, you just moved a knob

179
00:09:03,841 --> 00:09:09,140
or you pressed a button, and the machine had to do what you wanted to do.

180
00:09:09,141 --> 00:09:12,540
Spacewar spread from MIT to other computer labs

181
00:09:12,541 --> 00:09:15,420
and universities across the country on the ARPANET,

182
00:09:15,421 --> 00:09:17,120
the forerunner of the Internet.

183
00:09:17,121 --> 00:09:20,040
It was an instant hit among computer students.

184
00:09:20,041 --> 00:09:22,680
But Space War was unavailable to the public.

185
00:09:22,681 --> 00:09:25,480
After all, to play it, one needed a multimillion dollar

186
00:09:25,481 --> 00:09:26,920
mainframe computer.

187
00:09:26,921 --> 00:09:30,920
But the inspiration and innovation for a new generation

188
00:09:30,921 --> 00:09:33,480
of games was let loose on the world.

189
00:09:33,481 --> 00:09:35,320
It would turn a game like Space War,

190
00:09:35,321 --> 00:09:38,720
once a mainframe computer game, into an arcade staple

191
00:09:38,721 --> 00:09:42,320
in just 16 years by taming the technology.

192
00:09:42,321 --> 00:09:45,040
In the meantime, a new group of technologists,

193
00:09:45,041 --> 00:09:48,360
visionaries, and entrepreneurs pioneered the concept

194
00:09:48,361 --> 00:09:50,520
and development of video games.

195
00:09:50,620 --> 00:09:52,860
One was a military defense contractor.

196
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:55,100
The other, an arcade game entrepreneur.

197
00:09:59,900 --> 00:10:02,700
From Gettysburg to Desert Storm,

198
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:05,320
practically every decisive battle in history,

199
00:10:05,420 --> 00:10:07,520
on land, at sea, and in the air,

200
00:10:07,620 --> 00:10:10,120
has been turned into an interactive game.

201
00:10:10,220 --> 00:10:12,260
War games are an ever-popular genre

202
00:10:12,360 --> 00:10:16,220
and account for more than half the titles published in America.

203
00:10:16,320 --> 00:10:18,060
We have to accept that in our nature

204
00:10:18,061 --> 00:10:22,600
We're a somewhat imperialistic and predatory species,

205
00:10:22,700 --> 00:10:28,840
and we're attracted to dynamic, active experiences,

206
00:10:28,940 --> 00:10:30,240
war being one of them.

207
00:10:30,340 --> 00:10:31,740
One of the things about war games

208
00:10:31,840 --> 00:10:35,680
is that they take advantage of all of the intelligence

209
00:10:35,780 --> 00:10:39,020
and the strategic thinking and pattern recognition

210
00:10:39,120 --> 00:10:43,380
and many other aspects of how the human brain works uniquely.

211
00:10:43,480 --> 00:10:45,720
One of the most immersive games to be published

212
00:10:45,721 --> 00:10:49,560
simulates the Allied air battle over Europe in World War II.

213
00:10:49,660 --> 00:10:51,360
It's called flight combat.

214
00:10:51,460 --> 00:10:53,920
Players pilot a squadron of B-7 teams

215
00:10:54,020 --> 00:10:56,460
over the dangerous skies of Nazi Germany

216
00:10:56,560 --> 00:10:59,060
to blow up factories and win the war.

217
00:10:59,160 --> 00:11:02,160
Game developers were awestruck by the real history

218
00:11:02,260 --> 00:11:04,480
and decided it would make a great game.

219
00:11:04,580 --> 00:11:06,680
We had this idea about doing a World War II game

220
00:11:06,780 --> 00:11:08,880
and making it really immersive and focusing on a couple

221
00:11:08,980 --> 00:11:12,580
of very key features, like a 3D-driven physics-based

222
00:11:12,680 --> 00:11:15,720
damage system, where chunks would go flying off airplanes

223
00:11:15,721 --> 00:11:17,220
Guys would go spinning out.

224
00:11:17,221 --> 00:11:18,520
Engines would catch on fire.

225
00:11:18,521 --> 00:11:19,520
Planes would explode.

226
00:11:19,521 --> 00:11:21,520
It would be collateral damage, things like that.

227
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:27,240
But this is not just a game.

228
00:11:27,241 --> 00:11:30,440
It's an historical event where thousands perished.

229
00:11:30,441 --> 00:11:33,500
So the developers wrestled with technological, emotional,

230
00:11:33,501 --> 00:11:37,080
and ethical challenges in making it into a game.

231
00:11:37,081 --> 00:11:39,640
How far should they go in showing death?

232
00:11:39,641 --> 00:11:42,280
What kind of carnage is acceptable?

233
00:11:42,281 --> 00:11:45,420
To answer these questions, they asked veterans.

234
00:11:45,421 --> 00:11:51,980
I remember having Will Smiley, who was a B-17 pilot who flew about 28 missions in World War II.

235
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:58,260
He, you know, would, he was in here one afternoon for about four hours just sort of retelling his adventures.

236
00:11:59,460 --> 00:12:04,800
And he literally broke down and started crying when he was starting to think about his friends, the people that he'd lost.

237
00:12:04,801 --> 00:12:12,382
And, you know, that just, like, we had about 12 people in a room and, you know, we're all, like, tears are forming in our eyes and we're really feeling empathy for

238
00:12:12,383 --> 00:12:13,083
this person.

239
00:12:13,084 --> 00:12:15,580
And part of that is like relaying that in the game,

240
00:12:15,680 --> 00:12:17,420
is like how do you relay that empathy?

241
00:12:17,520 --> 00:12:22,380
How do you make that person feel like war is not a fun thing?

242
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:24,620
Even though flight combat is a game,

243
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:27,180
its developers wanted players to understand

244
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:29,020
that real war is not.

245
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:31,520
So they made it into a documentary with footage

246
00:12:31,620 --> 00:12:34,600
and information about what actually happened in the air

247
00:12:34,700 --> 00:12:37,900
war against Nazi Germany.

248
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,300
The sheer ambition of the technology

249
00:12:40,301 --> 00:12:44,440
technology in a game like flight combat makes it hard to imagine that the very

250
00:12:44,441 --> 00:12:48,940
first commercial video game was so primitive, so simple, it was a virtual

251
00:12:48,941 --> 00:12:53,920
electronic amoeba compared to today's dynamic games. Yet as mindless as that

252
00:12:53,921 --> 00:12:58,960
game may seem today, it was truly revolutionary and all it involved were

253
00:12:58,961 --> 00:13:03,040
two beams of light chasing each other across a cathode ray tube. The idea

254
00:13:03,041 --> 00:13:07,120
originated with a military defense contractor named Ralph Baer who built

255
00:13:07,121 --> 00:13:09,760
for the Apollo space missions.

256
00:13:09,860 --> 00:13:12,120
Baer's inspiration came as he stared into a bank

257
00:13:12,220 --> 00:13:15,420
of television sets at an airport in 1966.

258
00:13:15,520 --> 00:13:18,460
Everyone else saw arrival and departure schedules,

259
00:13:18,560 --> 00:13:20,460
but Baer saw the future.

260
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:23,880
He had a, you know, one of those eureka experiences.

261
00:13:23,980 --> 00:13:25,680
He's sitting looking at the television set,

262
00:13:25,780 --> 00:13:28,080
and he's saying, this television set is in millions

263
00:13:28,180 --> 00:13:30,180
upon millions of American homes,

264
00:13:30,280 --> 00:13:32,220
and all people are doing is looking at it.

265
00:13:32,320 --> 00:13:35,820
You know, there's got to be something else that we can do

266
00:13:35,821 --> 00:13:37,820
with the television.

267
00:13:37,821 --> 00:13:40,820
And I think he initially started off with trying to, like,

268
00:13:40,821 --> 00:13:42,820
etch a sketch kind of things, drawing things.

269
00:13:42,821 --> 00:13:45,820
And then he came up with the idea of playing games

270
00:13:45,821 --> 00:13:48,820
on the television set.

271
00:13:48,821 --> 00:13:51,820
Bear built an interface that moved a beam of light

272
00:13:51,821 --> 00:13:53,820
across a television set.

273
00:13:53,821 --> 00:13:56,820
From that day forward, there would be more to do with the TV

274
00:13:56,821 --> 00:13:58,820
than just watch it.

275
00:13:58,821 --> 00:14:03,820
It was kind of like the way people took back the TV screen.

276
00:14:03,821 --> 00:14:08,840
Because, like, the TV screen is something that's beamed at you by stations, by big stars and everything.

277
00:14:08,920 --> 00:14:14,040
And you just kind of sat there and watched it, you know, got a tan from the glow of the tube.

278
00:14:14,240 --> 00:14:20,340
And it was to realize you could actually affect something on that sacred screen, you know.

279
00:14:20,720 --> 00:14:25,340
That, you know, it was kind of like, just like you go to church and you never go up and, like, touch the altar, you know.

280
00:14:25,341 --> 00:14:30,940
And here you could actually go up and, you know, light the candles or, you know, break the candles.

281
00:14:35,114 --> 00:14:44,683
On August 10, 1970, Baer applied for patent $3,829.95 for an apparatus and method for the generation, display, and manipulation of symbols upon screens of television

282
00:14:44,684 --> 00:14:51,700
receivers for the purpose of training, simulation, playing games, and for engaging in action by one or more participants.

283
00:14:51,701 --> 00:14:56,700
At first, Bear took his brave new invention to the captains of cable television,

284
00:14:56,701 --> 00:15:00,700
the perfect delivery system, he thought, for playing video games.

285
00:15:00,701 --> 00:15:02,700
But cable wanted no part of it.

286
00:15:02,701 --> 00:15:05,700
Another company, however, did.

287
00:15:05,701 --> 00:15:12,700
In 1972, Magnavox released Bear's invention, a home game console named Odyssey.

288
00:15:12,701 --> 00:15:19,700
By adding a piece of plastic over a TV set, it became 12 different games, from table tennis to hockey.

289
00:15:19,701 --> 00:15:25,000
It was primitive by today's standards, but groundbreaking in its day and expensive.

290
00:15:25,380 --> 00:15:27,000
The system cost $100.

291
00:15:28,300 --> 00:15:32,760
Odyssey, the world's first home video game, was a moderate success.

292
00:15:33,280 --> 00:15:35,860
But another game took America by storm.

293
00:15:36,220 --> 00:15:42,260
It made a millionaire of its creator, inspired the idea of video arcades, and reinvented amusement.

294
00:15:42,780 --> 00:15:44,720
The world would never be the same.

295
00:15:49,700 --> 00:16:00,920
When it comes to games played at arcades, or at home on PCs or consoles, sports themes are a perennial favorite.

296
00:16:01,360 --> 00:16:06,820
There are games that duplicate every athletic event on the planet to evoke the fantasy of the major leagues.

297
00:16:06,821 --> 00:16:07,660
It has a chance.

298
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:11,640
A kind of thrill of virtual victory, an agony of virtual defeat.

299
00:16:11,940 --> 00:16:17,560
The sports, I think, in one sense, they're a metaphor for war, but they're also a metaphor for heroism,

300
00:16:17,561 --> 00:16:23,420
in that it creates a context in which it's socially acceptable to stand out from your peers and to achieve great things,

301
00:16:23,580 --> 00:16:27,940
and even to the point of having a crowd around you that's going to cheer at the moment when you do it.

302
00:16:28,940 --> 00:16:34,860
In the history of electronic games, it was a sporting event that inspired the first hit video game,

303
00:16:35,040 --> 00:16:41,200
but not because it offered a chance to play one-on-one with Michael Jordan or crush a homer off of Roger Clemens.

304
00:16:41,201 --> 00:16:47,900
It was a sports event because it was technically possible to create and both simple and fun to play.

305
00:16:48,340 --> 00:16:50,000
The game was Pong.

306
00:16:50,900 --> 00:16:55,000
It was like a test pattern, you know, going boop, boop, boop, boop.

307
00:16:55,240 --> 00:17:01,220
And basically everybody understood the analogy of ping pong, tennis, and it was just something cute to do.

308
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:07,040
And for the first time, a lot of people felt like they could drop a quarter in the machine and control a computer.

309
00:17:07,041 --> 00:17:11,540
and it was very cool to feel like you were playing a high-tech game.

310
00:17:12,440 --> 00:17:16,600
Pong was not a home game like the ones introduced by Ralph Baer the same year.

311
00:17:16,940 --> 00:17:19,780
Pong was a five-foot console built for arcades.

312
00:17:20,140 --> 00:17:22,680
The first two were leased to a gas station and a bar.

313
00:17:23,200 --> 00:17:26,040
They stood upright with a television monitor at eye level

314
00:17:26,041 --> 00:17:28,620
and used transistors, not microprocessors,

315
00:17:28,800 --> 00:17:31,520
to cast a beam of light on a black-and-white monitor.

316
00:17:33,400 --> 00:17:36,060
Pong was the brainchild of Nolan Bushnell,

317
00:17:36,061 --> 00:17:42,300
the founder of Atari, a company started on $250 borrowed dollars to make electronic arcade games

318
00:17:42,301 --> 00:17:45,807
and sold, less than a decade later, for $28 million.

319
00:17:45,808 --> 00:17:48,640
While a student at the University of Utah,

320
00:17:48,980 --> 00:17:50,461
Bushnell had played Space War.

321
00:17:50,462 --> 00:17:53,720
It was the world's first computer game, developed by Steve Russell

322
00:17:53,721 --> 00:17:56,985
at MIT and sent out over the first computer network.

323
00:17:56,986 --> 00:17:59,560
But Bushnell was not just into computers.

324
00:18:00,020 --> 00:18:03,482
He also loved games and worked nights at an amusement park.

325
00:18:03,483 --> 00:18:05,360
Bushnell believed computer games

326
00:18:05,361 --> 00:18:08,000
and arcades were destined to be together.

327
00:18:08,001 --> 00:18:10,560
But the creation of Pong was an accident.

328
00:18:10,561 --> 00:18:13,440
Their aim was initially to make a racing game,

329
00:18:13,441 --> 00:18:18,940
but they decided to test out their new programmer, designer,

330
00:18:18,941 --> 00:18:23,220
engineer, Al Alcorn, on a real simple table tennis game.

331
00:18:23,221 --> 00:18:25,420
And they realized that this is just incredibly fun.

332
00:18:25,421 --> 00:18:30,120
And they ended up with Pong, a game with only two instructions,

333
00:18:30,121 --> 00:18:33,220
insert coin and avoid missing ball for high score.

334
00:18:33,221 --> 00:18:36,960
And plus, you could play it with a beer in one hand.

335
00:18:36,961 --> 00:18:40,060
Pong hit the market in April 1972

336
00:18:40,061 --> 00:18:43,860
and quickly sold 6,000 units at $1,000 each.

337
00:18:43,861 --> 00:18:46,240
Now, standing next to pinball games,

338
00:18:46,241 --> 00:18:49,740
shooting galleries, and skeetball was Pong.

339
00:18:49,741 --> 00:18:52,080
But in less than a decade, pinball arcades

340
00:18:52,081 --> 00:18:54,480
would become video game arcades.

341
00:18:54,481 --> 00:18:56,240
The demand for Pong was so great,

342
00:18:56,241 --> 00:18:57,740
they couldn't keep up with it.

343
00:18:57,741 --> 00:19:02,480
And the games overflowed with quarters, jamming machines.

344
00:19:02,481 --> 00:19:06,080
Pong was a Neanderthal compared to today's sports games,

345
00:19:06,081 --> 00:19:08,320
but it was the first step in an evolution

346
00:19:08,321 --> 00:19:11,360
to virtual reality that was achieved one breakthrough

347
00:19:11,361 --> 00:19:12,320
at a time.

348
00:19:12,321 --> 00:19:15,720
The first advance after Pong's transistor-based technology

349
00:19:15,721 --> 00:19:19,160
was the use of a ROM chip to store graphics in a game called

350
00:19:19,161 --> 00:19:21,260
Tank in 1974.

351
00:19:21,261 --> 00:19:24,440
Now, for the very first time, an object actually

352
00:19:24,441 --> 00:19:28,000
looked like something, not just a stick figure.

353
00:19:28,001 --> 00:19:31,000
After Pong and Tank, the technological crusade

354
00:19:31,001 --> 00:19:34,200
was to get more data, more bits on the screen.

355
00:19:34,201 --> 00:19:36,300
Insiders called it the bit revolution.

356
00:19:41,080 --> 00:19:44,480
That basically means that you can get more data through,

357
00:19:44,481 --> 00:19:48,160
you know, in a fraction of a second than you could before.

358
00:19:48,161 --> 00:19:51,280
All your throughput to have dots bouncing against each other

359
00:19:51,281 --> 00:19:55,020
in the Pong days, and then to have blocky polygons moving

360
00:19:55,021 --> 00:19:59,160
in the 8-bit days, and then to be able to have more colors

361
00:19:59,161 --> 00:20:02,800
in the 16-bit days, and then to be able to have more polygons

362
00:20:02,801 --> 00:20:06,320
and textures in the 32-bit days and the 64-bit days,

363
00:20:06,321 --> 00:20:09,320
all that really means is that you're pushing more information

364
00:20:09,321 --> 00:20:12,120
onto the screen and that everything looks brighter

365
00:20:12,121 --> 00:20:16,480
and bigger and more realistic and more detailed.

366
00:20:16,481 --> 00:20:19,940
After the bit revolution came the color revolution.

367
00:20:19,941 --> 00:20:22,920
Monitors, once capable only of amber or green,

368
00:20:22,921 --> 00:20:24,600
burst out in color.

369
00:20:24,601 --> 00:20:27,500
By 1976, that spelled a thankful end

370
00:20:27,501 --> 00:20:29,800
to black and white games.

371
00:20:29,801 --> 00:20:33,000
Breakout was probably the first big color game,

372
00:20:33,001 --> 00:20:36,600
and it was the one that really got the Atari machines

373
00:20:36,601 --> 00:20:39,560
into Sears, and I think that's when it really took off

374
00:20:39,561 --> 00:20:41,360
as a mass market.

375
00:20:41,361 --> 00:20:44,560
Breakout was a breakthrough, but its real impact

376
00:20:44,561 --> 00:20:47,800
was in helping start the personal computer revolution.

377
00:20:47,801 --> 00:20:52,240
Its young designers went on to start a company called Apple.

378
00:20:52,241 --> 00:20:55,840
Breakout in 76 was designed by Steve Jobs

379
00:20:55,841 --> 00:20:58,840
who went on to found Apple Computer after that.

380
00:20:58,940 --> 00:21:03,040
And the Apple One prototype shared a lot of the parts

381
00:21:03,140 --> 00:21:06,380
with the breakout architecture because they stole a lot

382
00:21:06,480 --> 00:21:07,780
from Atari at the time.

383
00:21:11,080 --> 00:21:13,400
The advent of color moved video games

384
00:21:13,500 --> 00:21:15,900
off the back road of black and white stick figures

385
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,800
and onto the highway toward photo realism.

386
00:21:18,900 --> 00:21:21,800
But another Atari innovation gave the game world

387
00:21:21,900 --> 00:21:24,600
a new tool for play and an icon.

388
00:21:24,601 --> 00:21:26,400
It was the joystick.

389
00:21:26,500 --> 00:21:31,400
The joystick first came with a 1977 Atari 2600 home game

390
00:21:31,500 --> 00:21:32,300
console.

391
00:21:32,400 --> 00:21:35,440
It also featured a breakthrough 8-bit central processor

392
00:21:35,540 --> 00:21:36,340
unit.

393
00:21:36,440 --> 00:21:38,840
The joystick was similar to a computer mouse,

394
00:21:38,940 --> 00:21:40,940
but instead of manipulating a cursor,

395
00:21:41,040 --> 00:21:43,360
it moved an object or fired a shot.

396
00:21:43,460 --> 00:21:46,360
The joystick gave the player more control over the game

397
00:21:46,460 --> 00:21:48,800
and a sense of being in the action.

398
00:21:48,900 --> 00:21:52,200
You know, you might want to lighten up someday, Slade.

399
00:21:52,201 --> 00:21:56,200
I hear being an all the time can raise your cholesterol level.

400
00:21:56,201 --> 00:21:59,200
But hardware aside, like joysticks,

401
00:21:59,201 --> 00:22:02,200
bits, and color monitors, the next big thing in games

402
00:22:02,201 --> 00:22:05,200
was adding a storyline and role-playing.

403
00:22:05,201 --> 00:22:08,200
Although role-play and story was a primitive idea

404
00:22:08,201 --> 00:22:12,200
to games in the 1970s, it came to be the raison d'etre

405
00:22:12,201 --> 00:22:15,200
for Millennium Games, and it started with a game

406
00:22:15,201 --> 00:22:17,200
called Space Invaders.

407
00:22:17,201 --> 00:22:22,200
I had this block of invaders, this array of invaders,

408
00:22:22,300 --> 00:22:24,200
and as you kept whittling away at them,

409
00:22:24,300 --> 00:22:25,800
blowing away creature after creature,

410
00:22:25,900 --> 00:22:27,940
they kept going faster and faster and faster

411
00:22:28,040 --> 00:22:30,540
and descending on you, and the heartbeat got faster

412
00:22:30,640 --> 00:22:34,160
and faster, and it was enough to drive you nuts.

413
00:22:37,860 --> 00:22:39,660
It was an astounding experience.

414
00:22:39,760 --> 00:22:42,900
You were, for the very first time, somewhere else

415
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,900
fighting something else, and it was life and death.

416
00:22:45,901 --> 00:22:47,900
It really was an amazing experience.

417
00:22:47,901 --> 00:22:49,900
Space Invaders was so popular,

418
00:22:49,901 --> 00:22:53,900
it caused a coin shortage in Japan and truancy in America.

419
00:22:53,901 --> 00:22:57,900
It also changed how people thought about electronic games.

420
00:22:57,901 --> 00:23:01,900
Space Invaders started this whole, to me,

421
00:23:01,901 --> 00:23:04,900
the whole genre of artificial intelligence.

422
00:23:04,901 --> 00:23:06,900
Because you had an enemy,

423
00:23:06,901 --> 00:23:10,900
the first game that actually had computer enemies on the screen

424
00:23:10,901 --> 00:23:13,900
that had their own little life,

425
00:23:13,901 --> 00:23:16,400
They had their own objectives, had their own goals.

426
00:23:16,500 --> 00:23:18,900
And it was kind of you, either you would survive

427
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:20,400
or they would survive.

428
00:23:20,500 --> 00:23:24,440
And to me, that was just, it was just like this incredible

429
00:23:24,540 --> 00:23:27,840
struggle which always ended in your defeat.

430
00:23:27,940 --> 00:23:30,960
But for some reason, you had to keep struggling against it.

431
00:23:31,060 --> 00:23:33,260
Space Invaders was so popular,

432
00:23:33,360 --> 00:23:37,360
it turned arcades into the new dens of iniquity for teenagers.

433
00:23:37,460 --> 00:23:40,460
Even though home consoles offered many of the same titles,

434
00:23:40,560 --> 00:23:43,160
they didn't have the same high-tech performance.

435
00:23:43,161 --> 00:23:46,160
Arcade games were bigger, more powerful, and louder.

436
00:23:46,260 --> 00:23:49,000
They were packed with thousands of dollars of technology,

437
00:23:49,100 --> 00:23:50,960
paid for by millions of kids,

438
00:23:51,060 --> 00:23:53,800
pumping billions of quarters into the video slots

439
00:23:53,900 --> 00:23:55,800
to take on aliens.

440
00:23:55,900 --> 00:23:58,300
But soon they sparked an international outcry

441
00:23:58,400 --> 00:24:01,620
that dubbed them the electronic drug.

442
00:24:01,720 --> 00:24:04,220
In the Philippines, Ferdinand Marcos banned them.

443
00:24:04,320 --> 00:24:06,720
In America, city councils enacted curfews

444
00:24:06,820 --> 00:24:08,460
to make children come home.

445
00:24:08,560 --> 00:24:10,260
The Surgeon General, C. Everett Cook,

446
00:24:10,360 --> 00:24:12,320
who ordered warning labels on cigarettes,

447
00:24:12,321 --> 00:24:17,980
cigarettes said video games produced aberrations in childhood behavior, causing users to be

448
00:24:17,981 --> 00:24:20,100
addicted body and soul.

449
00:24:20,101 --> 00:24:24,100
Even the Journal of American Medicine announced that video game play damaged wrists and hands

450
00:24:24,101 --> 00:24:25,240
over time.

451
00:24:25,241 --> 00:24:27,040
But nothing kept the kids from playing.

452
00:24:27,041 --> 00:24:29,180
Oh, there's a flying saucer.

453
00:24:29,181 --> 00:24:32,320
Kids are naturally drawn toward things that shock and offend adults.

454
00:24:32,321 --> 00:24:38,640
I think what we're afraid of about these games is that they bring to the surface dark sides

455
00:24:38,641 --> 00:24:41,640
of our children, aggressive side of our children,

456
00:24:41,641 --> 00:24:44,580
and children's sexual fantasies.

457
00:24:44,581 --> 00:24:46,220
We don't like to accept that children

458
00:24:46,221 --> 00:24:48,340
have any of those things.

459
00:24:48,341 --> 00:24:52,180
Loved or loathed, more games followed in new genres,

460
00:24:52,181 --> 00:24:56,500
like Lunar Lander, the first flight simulation game.

461
00:24:56,501 --> 00:24:59,500
Then Asteroids, the last great black and white

462
00:24:59,501 --> 00:25:01,660
two-dimensional game.

463
00:25:01,661 --> 00:25:05,640
Then came a game destined to be the video icon of the 80s.

464
00:25:05,641 --> 00:25:08,240
It was derived from a Japanese nursery rhyme

465
00:25:08,241 --> 00:25:09,980
about a devouring monster.

466
00:25:09,981 --> 00:25:11,640
It was called Pac-Man.

467
00:25:12,940 --> 00:25:14,840
Pac-Man was followed by Defender,

468
00:25:14,841 --> 00:25:16,620
a game that left an indelible mark

469
00:25:16,621 --> 00:25:17,980
on the generation of gamers

470
00:25:17,981 --> 00:25:20,560
because of its sheer emotional intensity.

471
00:25:20,561 --> 00:25:24,260
The first game to achieve so visceral a response.

472
00:25:24,261 --> 00:25:26,620
All we had was this title, Defender.

473
00:25:26,621 --> 00:25:28,060
You know, like you're defending something

474
00:25:28,061 --> 00:25:30,760
because if you want to blow up things

475
00:25:30,761 --> 00:25:33,100
and show a lot of gratuitous violence,

476
00:25:33,101 --> 00:25:35,200
it's nice to have the moral high ground.

477
00:25:35,201 --> 00:25:37,760
You know, you're defending the world.

478
00:25:37,761 --> 00:25:40,300
You're not just raping and pillaging,

479
00:25:40,301 --> 00:25:42,540
but you're on the side of fear.

480
00:25:42,541 --> 00:25:44,400
Defender was one of the best ways

481
00:25:44,401 --> 00:25:46,260
that I could probably spend my allowance.

482
00:25:46,261 --> 00:25:48,880
These poor people were being taken away by the aliens,

483
00:25:48,881 --> 00:25:50,580
and your job was to shoot the aliens

484
00:25:50,581 --> 00:25:53,600
so your poor settlers or native population

485
00:25:53,601 --> 00:25:55,940
wouldn't be swept away and kidnapped.

486
00:25:55,941 --> 00:25:57,540
The popularity of video games

487
00:25:57,541 --> 00:25:59,320
reached every part of America,

488
00:25:59,321 --> 00:26:02,620
even the Oval Office and President Ronald Reagan.

489
00:26:02,621 --> 00:26:04,760
Once you begin a great movement,

490
00:26:04,761 --> 00:26:06,760
there's no telling where it'll end.

491
00:26:06,761 --> 00:26:15,960
He talked about how these video games were training, you know, the warriors of the next century to, you know, in a positive fashion.

492
00:26:15,961 --> 00:26:26,942
I mean, he did not, this was not as to where today you have people saying, you know, calling video games ridiculously, merger simulators and things like that, flying

493
00:26:26,943 --> 00:26:27,940
off the handle.

494
00:26:28,020 --> 00:26:32,380
He was just talking about how they were developing the skills that would be needed in the future.

495
00:26:32,700 --> 00:26:35,500
And, you know, he wasn't so far off.

496
00:26:35,501 --> 00:26:39,300
For all the leaps in technology that boosted the sophistication

497
00:26:39,400 --> 00:26:43,140
of video games, none compared to the one about to change America

498
00:26:43,240 --> 00:26:45,040
and video games as well.

499
00:26:45,140 --> 00:26:48,240
The games, played at arcades and on home consoles,

500
00:26:48,340 --> 00:26:51,280
were about to have yet another platform to play on,

501
00:26:51,380 --> 00:26:52,960
the home computer.

502
00:26:53,060 --> 00:26:55,660
And from the very first Apple and IBM,

503
00:26:55,760 --> 00:26:59,160
the world of video games was about to become a home staple.

504
00:26:59,161 --> 00:27:00,000
a home staple.

505
00:27:03,340 --> 00:27:08,460
Ladies and gentlemen, boys and girls,

506
00:27:08,461 --> 00:27:11,300
let's get ready to rumble.

507
00:27:11,301 --> 00:27:14,920
By the mid-1980s, video games had become an entertainment

508
00:27:14,921 --> 00:27:15,640
icon.

509
00:27:15,641 --> 00:27:17,920
In honor of its place in the American heart,

510
00:27:17,921 --> 00:27:21,600
the industry received the ultimate pop tribute.

511
00:27:21,601 --> 00:27:26,060
In 1999, the US Post Office issued a commemorative stamp

512
00:27:26,061 --> 00:27:27,320
in its honor.

513
00:27:27,321 --> 00:27:28,260
And why not?

514
00:27:28,261 --> 00:27:30,800
Video games were right up there among the best ideas

515
00:27:30,801 --> 00:27:32,280
America had ever come up with.

516
00:27:32,720 --> 00:27:36,780
They were original, fun, fast, hip, and high tech.

517
00:27:37,140 --> 00:27:39,560
And most of all, they were cool.

518
00:27:40,600 --> 00:27:42,820
On their way to the heights of popularity,

519
00:27:43,140 --> 00:27:44,660
these games did more than amuse.

520
00:27:45,020 --> 00:27:48,480
They also addressed a new generation's psychological needs,

521
00:27:48,481 --> 00:27:51,460
and they did so as easily as hitting an on-off switch.

522
00:27:52,540 --> 00:27:56,580
We have all these inner fantasies of, like, taking charge,

523
00:27:56,581 --> 00:27:59,220
you know, Rambo-esque things of, you know,

524
00:27:59,280 --> 00:28:00,660
making things right, you know?

525
00:28:00,680 --> 00:28:03,180
It's like we sure screwed up, you know, the real world,

526
00:28:03,560 --> 00:28:05,960
so at least in the video game, maybe we can fix things there.

527
00:28:06,400 --> 00:28:08,920
You know, we can make it right in the artificial world,

528
00:28:09,560 --> 00:28:11,500
you know, because the real world is just way too messy to do.

529
00:28:14,200 --> 00:28:16,800
One of the founders of the industry was Atari.

530
00:28:17,140 --> 00:28:19,480
Their games were so successful at the arcade

531
00:28:19,481 --> 00:28:22,280
and in home renditions that a flood of competitors followed,

532
00:28:22,600 --> 00:28:25,800
many from Japan, like Nintendo, Sega, and Sony.

533
00:28:25,801 --> 00:28:31,800
Though masters of arcade games, they left a mark on home consoles, systems that operate

534
00:28:31,801 --> 00:28:36,640
like miniature arcades feeding a television with a cartridge or CD-ROM.

535
00:28:36,641 --> 00:28:41,380
The consoles were the first platforms to take games into a third dimension at home by adding

536
00:28:41,381 --> 00:28:45,800
superior graphics that left wireframe two-dimension behind.

537
00:28:45,801 --> 00:28:53,560
Photorealism in video games and computer games is the holy grail.

538
00:28:53,561 --> 00:28:57,360
All of a sudden you say, whoa, that's like real life,

539
00:28:57,460 --> 00:29:01,360
but impossible, and I like that.

540
00:29:01,460 --> 00:29:04,300
Nintendo, whose name means luck of heaven,

541
00:29:04,400 --> 00:29:07,100
went from a maker of playing cards in Japan

542
00:29:07,200 --> 00:29:10,040
to become a global master of arcade games.

543
00:29:10,140 --> 00:29:13,020
They brought Donkey Kong and Mario to the world

544
00:29:13,120 --> 00:29:15,240
and miniaturized the concept of gameplay

545
00:29:15,340 --> 00:29:18,120
in handheld units called Game Boys.

546
00:29:18,220 --> 00:29:20,920
Their game success is legendary.

547
00:29:20,921 --> 00:29:24,120
Nintendo's Super Mario 3 sold more copies

548
00:29:24,220 --> 00:29:28,120
than any other video game in history, 7 million.

549
00:29:28,220 --> 00:29:32,160
In one year, 1992, pre-tax profits for Nintendo

550
00:29:32,260 --> 00:29:34,460
was $7 billion.

551
00:29:34,560 --> 00:29:38,280
By the 90s, Nintendo beat all of Hollywood in earned revenue.

552
00:29:38,380 --> 00:29:42,100
They moved video games from a fad to a staple of play.

553
00:29:42,200 --> 00:29:44,480
In the process, they created a generation

554
00:29:44,580 --> 00:29:46,920
of revolutionary home game consoles

555
00:29:47,020 --> 00:29:50,920
that reached an apogee in the Nintendo 64.

556
00:29:50,921 --> 00:29:54,160
But Nintendo's success only inspired more competition

557
00:29:54,161 --> 00:29:55,820
in games and hardware technology.

558
00:29:56,260 --> 00:29:58,440
Video games were not just for the kiddies anymore.

559
00:29:58,880 --> 00:30:02,400
By the mid-1990s, the average age of a gamer was 24.

560
00:30:03,020 --> 00:30:05,280
A PlayStation game console could be found

561
00:30:05,281 --> 00:30:07,940
in one out of every four U.S. households.

562
00:30:08,900 --> 00:30:12,320
Sega and Sony continued to push the envelope of technology.

563
00:30:12,920 --> 00:30:15,920
Sega's Dreamcast's American release in 1999

564
00:30:15,921 --> 00:30:19,980
sold 250,000 units within 24 hours

565
00:30:19,981 --> 00:30:22,180
and $400,000 in five days.

566
00:30:23,280 --> 00:30:26,740
Sega Dreamcast is responsible for the single largest day

567
00:30:26,741 --> 00:30:29,760
in retail of any kind of entertainment experience ever.

568
00:30:30,120 --> 00:30:33,560
$97 million in one single day of sales.

569
00:30:33,760 --> 00:30:36,920
I mean, it was amazing because we tried to look at

570
00:30:36,921 --> 00:30:40,100
what was the biggest retail event up until that time.

571
00:30:40,320 --> 00:30:41,600
And we had to go beyond retail.

572
00:30:41,720 --> 00:30:43,620
We had to look into all forms of entertainment.

573
00:30:43,621 --> 00:30:45,640
And we looked at sales of CDs.

574
00:30:45,641 --> 00:30:50,440
And we finally found the Phantom Menace at 28.2 million

575
00:30:50,540 --> 00:30:51,980
was the single largest event.

576
00:30:52,080 --> 00:30:54,380
And we blew that away almost three times.

577
00:30:54,480 --> 00:30:57,880
Remember, I'm the monkey and you're the cheese grater.

578
00:30:57,980 --> 00:30:59,180
So no messing around.

579
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:04,320
But the next generation of home consoles

580
00:31:04,420 --> 00:31:07,600
will do more than elevate games to new graphic levels.

581
00:31:07,700 --> 00:31:10,640
Gaming consoles like Sony's PlayStation 2

582
00:31:10,740 --> 00:31:13,500
will become the essence of the home entertainment center,

583
00:31:13,501 --> 00:31:15,900
combining various mediums of entertainment.

584
00:31:15,901 --> 00:31:18,180
It'll play video games and DVDs

585
00:31:18,181 --> 00:31:21,540
and replace the personal computer for an internet connection.

586
00:31:21,541 --> 00:31:23,940
While the game hardware becomes more sophisticated,

587
00:31:23,941 --> 00:31:27,780
the game's software is changing the definition of interaction.

588
00:31:30,620 --> 00:31:32,620
You blow something up, you know you've interacted.

589
00:31:32,621 --> 00:31:34,060
You know that you've pushed the button

590
00:31:34,061 --> 00:31:35,580
and it's made a difference.

591
00:31:35,581 --> 00:31:38,660
You know that your participation is actively changing

592
00:31:38,661 --> 00:31:40,620
the environment which is there on the screen.

593
00:31:40,621 --> 00:31:42,660
But it becomes almost too easy to offer

594
00:31:42,661 --> 00:31:44,900
that blowing things up over and over again

595
00:31:45,000 --> 00:31:50,660
as the only kind of satisfaction offered to game players.

596
00:31:50,760 --> 00:31:52,600
One of the most dramatic examples

597
00:31:52,700 --> 00:31:56,300
of the rethinking of Interaction is a new game from Sega.

598
00:31:56,400 --> 00:31:59,620
It cost $60 million, the most ever spent,

599
00:31:59,720 --> 00:32:02,120
to develop Shenmue, a new milestone

600
00:32:02,220 --> 00:32:04,420
in the meaning of Interaction.

601
00:32:04,520 --> 00:32:07,060
Shenmue's an example of really what

602
00:32:07,160 --> 00:32:08,720
can be looked at in the future.

603
00:32:08,820 --> 00:32:11,120
You actually go up and you talk to a character.

604
00:32:11,121 --> 00:32:12,620
And when you talk to that character,

605
00:32:12,621 --> 00:32:13,960
depending on the type of day,

606
00:32:13,961 --> 00:32:16,120
depending on what you say to that character,

607
00:32:16,121 --> 00:32:18,620
will cause a different sort of reaction to come back to.

608
00:32:18,621 --> 00:32:22,160
They have lots of stuff that will bring back childhood memories.

609
00:32:22,161 --> 00:32:23,860
It's really fun.

610
00:32:23,861 --> 00:32:26,160
It goes beyond just wanting to kill something

611
00:32:26,161 --> 00:32:27,880
and get the points for killing them,

612
00:32:27,881 --> 00:32:29,380
that they might actually have information

613
00:32:29,381 --> 00:32:32,680
or that the relationship you have is valuable in the future.

614
00:32:32,681 --> 00:32:34,780
The games are still in their infancy,

615
00:32:34,781 --> 00:32:36,480
but the technology behind them

616
00:32:36,481 --> 00:32:38,320
has raised the excitement of games

617
00:32:38,321 --> 00:32:41,520
from eye-hand challenges to the level, some would say,

618
00:32:41,620 --> 00:32:43,560
of art and literature.

619
00:32:43,660 --> 00:32:47,820
The technology is so good, so complete, it's invisible.

620
00:32:47,920 --> 00:32:51,300
Games can now focus on creating a whole new experience,

621
00:32:51,400 --> 00:32:55,000
far beyond adrenaline-pumping thrills and over-the-top sound.

622
00:32:55,100 --> 00:32:57,600
And the vehicle to take games to the next level

623
00:32:57,700 --> 00:33:00,980
is the technology of the personal computer and the Internet.

624
00:33:08,320 --> 00:33:22,320
Electronic games were born amid the computer revolution of the 1960s.

625
00:33:22,340 --> 00:33:26,660
By 1981, when two former Atari game developers started Apple,

626
00:33:26,980 --> 00:33:29,380
games were added to the world's first personal computer.

627
00:33:29,920 --> 00:33:31,600
IBM followed suit a year later,

628
00:33:31,740 --> 00:33:34,120
going so far as to add a joystick to their PC.

629
00:33:34,121 --> 00:33:37,160
What no one imagined was that computers would advance

630
00:33:37,260 --> 00:33:39,660
the development of games at warp speed

631
00:33:39,760 --> 00:33:41,920
and become one of the most important platforms

632
00:33:42,020 --> 00:33:44,160
to play them on, getting more sophisticated

633
00:33:44,260 --> 00:33:45,860
with every breakthrough in technology,

634
00:33:45,960 --> 00:33:48,660
from microprocessors to memory.

635
00:33:48,760 --> 00:33:51,340
While arcade players and home consoles

636
00:33:51,440 --> 00:33:53,480
dominated the medium in the early days,

637
00:33:53,580 --> 00:33:56,680
it was only a matter of time before a home computer's

638
00:33:56,780 --> 00:34:00,080
price and performance made it the game platform in millions

639
00:34:00,180 --> 00:34:01,480
of homes around the world.

640
00:34:01,481 --> 00:34:08,480
world.

641
00:34:08,481 --> 00:34:10,980
Video games brought computers into the culture.

642
00:34:10,981 --> 00:34:14,360
It, it, uh, they brought computers into the home.

643
00:34:14,361 --> 00:34:17,500
And I mean, you know, hey, do you need 16.7 million colors

644
00:34:17,501 --> 00:34:19,600
on your desktop to do a spreadsheet?

645
00:34:19,601 --> 00:34:22,260
Do you need 32-bit stereo sound for word processing?

646
00:34:22,261 --> 00:34:23,900
No, absolutely not.

647
00:34:23,901 --> 00:34:25,880
You need them for video games, and that's what people

648
00:34:25,881 --> 00:34:26,980
buy computers for.

649
00:34:31,480 --> 00:34:44,960
With PC usage growing in more and more homes,

650
00:34:45,060 --> 00:34:48,160
the days of the arcade as the place to play electronic games

651
00:34:48,260 --> 00:34:49,260
were numbered.

652
00:34:49,360 --> 00:34:51,700
Instead of feeding quarters into a video slot,

653
00:34:51,800 --> 00:34:55,040
kids were urging their parents to buy a family computer,

654
00:34:55,140 --> 00:34:58,500
but with all the power possible to drive a hot new 3D graphic

655
00:34:58,600 --> 00:34:59,900
game.

656
00:34:59,901 --> 00:35:02,740
Computer is the medium of doing,

657
00:35:02,840 --> 00:35:06,400
and people learn by doing, and we love to play.

658
00:35:06,500 --> 00:35:08,000
And it's just a perfect fit.

659
00:35:10,240 --> 00:35:13,340
At first, computer games were copies or extensions

660
00:35:13,440 --> 00:35:15,040
of arcade and console games.

661
00:35:15,140 --> 00:35:16,960
This is my shape.

662
00:35:17,060 --> 00:35:19,220
But by the mid-'90s, they began to find

663
00:35:19,320 --> 00:35:21,660
their own unique distinction as games.

664
00:35:21,760 --> 00:35:23,860
Central to their identity was an emphasis

665
00:35:23,960 --> 00:35:25,600
on more mature tastes.

666
00:35:25,700 --> 00:35:28,600
Will you dedicate yourself to our survival?

667
00:35:29,900 --> 00:35:35,600
The early games, quite frankly, were just classic hack and slash, you know, generic swords and sorcery.

668
00:35:36,160 --> 00:35:40,520
But over time, I began to get feedback that you might consider things like fan mail,

669
00:35:41,040 --> 00:35:45,760
or I began to see the impact that these games were having on, you know, young people around the world.

670
00:35:46,380 --> 00:35:51,700
And I personalized that to saying that, you know, with this opportunity to speak to the masses,

671
00:35:51,960 --> 00:35:59,360
came a responsibility to ensure that the content that they were participating in was content that had a net positive effect.

672
00:35:59,900 --> 00:36:03,500
Far from the days of a beam of light ricocheting off rackets,

673
00:36:03,600 --> 00:36:05,500
computer and video games have advanced

674
00:36:05,600 --> 00:36:08,500
into complex storylines, complete with heroes,

675
00:36:08,600 --> 00:36:11,340
villains, plots, scenes, and settings,

676
00:36:11,440 --> 00:36:15,040
anywhere from the portals of hell to the tombs of Egypt.

677
00:36:15,140 --> 00:36:17,960
The computer game is now a medium for stories.

678
00:36:20,660 --> 00:36:23,760
We're at the threshold of a new era of storytelling.

679
00:36:23,860 --> 00:36:25,560
One can imagine the future.

680
00:36:25,660 --> 00:36:28,000
We look back and we're talking about the cyber bards,

681
00:36:28,001 --> 00:36:30,000
that is the storytellers who have grabbed hold

682
00:36:30,001 --> 00:36:34,000
of the potentials of an immersive media technology

683
00:36:34,001 --> 00:36:38,000
and really begun to explore how one can use that

684
00:36:38,001 --> 00:36:39,000
to tell stories.

685
00:36:39,001 --> 00:36:40,000
We're at a baby stage.

686
00:36:40,001 --> 00:36:43,000
But down the line, we may see it as an exciting

687
00:36:43,001 --> 00:36:47,000
and dynamic a form as cinema itself.

688
00:36:47,001 --> 00:36:50,000
We want to develop a game that engages and challenges

689
00:36:50,001 --> 00:36:53,000
and inspires, but most important, inspires.

690
00:36:53,001 --> 00:36:55,000
And what do we mean by that?

691
00:36:55,001 --> 00:36:57,000
That means that it's not only inspires you to keep

692
00:36:57,001 --> 00:36:58,960
inspires you to keep playing.

693
00:36:58,961 --> 00:37:00,900
It inspires you to want to know more about it,

694
00:37:00,901 --> 00:37:03,740
want to be more immersed in the characters,

695
00:37:03,741 --> 00:37:09,040
be more sympathetic to them so that when they hurt, you hurt.

696
00:37:09,041 --> 00:37:10,180
That becomes very important.

697
00:37:10,181 --> 00:37:15,620
It's really finding a way to connect the player

698
00:37:15,621 --> 00:37:19,020
with that main character, with the game,

699
00:37:19,021 --> 00:37:21,920
so that they feel each other's pain.

700
00:37:21,921 --> 00:37:24,200
If you haven't bagged a premium before the next sales

701
00:37:24,201 --> 00:37:26,200
comes in, you're out!

702
00:37:26,300 --> 00:37:28,000
Guess they couldn't save me, huh?

703
00:37:28,100 --> 00:37:31,600
No, but there's still a chance you could save me.

704
00:37:31,700 --> 00:37:33,700
Hollywood has flocked to computer games

705
00:37:33,800 --> 00:37:35,940
as a new creative storytelling outlet,

706
00:37:36,040 --> 00:37:38,640
attracting some of the biggest names in film.

707
00:37:38,740 --> 00:37:41,020
Steven Spielberg turned the epic blockbuster

708
00:37:41,120 --> 00:37:44,420
Saving Private Ryan into the game Medal of Honor.

709
00:37:44,520 --> 00:37:47,960
Tom Clancy was behind Rainbow Six and Rogue Spear.

710
00:37:48,060 --> 00:37:51,160
And the granddaddy of science fiction and movie myth,

711
00:37:51,161 --> 00:37:55,160
George Lucas has an entire company at work making games.

712
00:37:59,160 --> 00:38:02,160
While home computers have shaped games into stories,

713
00:38:02,161 --> 00:38:06,160
the advent of the Internet and online game play

714
00:38:06,161 --> 00:38:09,160
have turned games into a kind of parallel universe,

715
00:38:09,161 --> 00:38:11,160
where people not only play games,

716
00:38:11,161 --> 00:38:13,160
they explore aspects of themselves,

717
00:38:13,161 --> 00:38:16,160
a kind of exercise in conscious living,

718
00:38:16,161 --> 00:38:19,160
free from the stresses of the real world.

719
00:38:19,161 --> 00:38:23,060
One of the most popular online games is the Ultima series.

720
00:38:23,160 --> 00:38:25,660
It's a medieval fantasy world.

721
00:38:25,760 --> 00:38:28,160
But more than a game, it's a community.

722
00:38:28,260 --> 00:38:30,600
The game connects people around the globe

723
00:38:30,700 --> 00:38:33,300
drawn to a similar game culture and experience.

724
00:38:33,400 --> 00:38:36,480
Unlike other games that involve amassing a body count,

725
00:38:36,580 --> 00:38:40,040
this emphasizes character and ethics, parts of the game

726
00:38:40,140 --> 00:38:41,620
that have made it a bestseller.

727
00:38:44,260 --> 00:38:47,080
I began to create a game where you had to prove yourself

728
00:38:47,081 --> 00:38:49,320
to prove yourself to be a person of good virtue

729
00:38:49,420 --> 00:38:52,520
in order to win the game, not just overcome the obstacles.

730
00:38:52,620 --> 00:38:55,820
People knew whenever they played a Lord British game

731
00:38:55,920 --> 00:38:59,020
that they were going to be immersed in the world of Britannia

732
00:38:59,120 --> 00:39:01,560
and they knew that they were going to face things

733
00:39:01,660 --> 00:39:04,260
that they had never faced before.

734
00:39:04,360 --> 00:39:07,540
Because everything that you did, every person that you killed,

735
00:39:07,640 --> 00:39:09,600
every item that you stole, you know,

736
00:39:09,700 --> 00:39:11,980
worked against your karma, if you will.

737
00:39:12,080 --> 00:39:15,940
And basically, you had to work on being the balanced avatar.

738
00:39:15,941 --> 00:39:18,640
You not only had to save the world, but you had to save yourself.

739
00:39:20,340 --> 00:39:24,740
My lord, the Avatar has arrived in Britannia

740
00:39:24,741 --> 00:39:28,700
and has been easily dispatched by my worm guard.

741
00:39:29,160 --> 00:39:30,580
Do not be so sure.

742
00:39:31,540 --> 00:39:35,900
The meddlesome champion of virtue is not so easily defeated.

743
00:39:36,500 --> 00:39:39,440
With the advent of the internet, now we finally have the ability

744
00:39:39,441 --> 00:39:43,640
for people to sit in their own homes, connect together via the internet

745
00:39:43,641 --> 00:39:45,480
so that everyone who plays this game

746
00:39:45,580 --> 00:39:48,140
is simultaneously connected together.

747
00:39:48,240 --> 00:39:51,840
At any point in time, up to 200,000 people around the world

748
00:39:51,940 --> 00:39:53,680
are online playing Ultima.

749
00:39:53,780 --> 00:39:55,780
The game is updated and changed online

750
00:39:55,880 --> 00:39:59,080
when a new version is ready, so there are no obstacles or costs

751
00:39:59,180 --> 00:40:01,300
associated with packaging or shipping.

752
00:40:01,400 --> 00:40:04,400
Like a utility bill for water or electricity,

753
00:40:04,500 --> 00:40:07,700
players pay a monthly fee to enter a virtual world

754
00:40:07,800 --> 00:40:09,840
where there are permanent consequences,

755
00:40:09,841 --> 00:40:13,840
a world that never ends, and a game that is never over.

756
00:40:13,841 --> 00:40:17,840
What you do here lasts your entire virtual lifetime.

757
00:40:17,841 --> 00:40:20,840
The game has taken off beyond the wildest imagination

758
00:40:20,841 --> 00:40:23,840
of its creator, and now includes people paying real money

759
00:40:23,841 --> 00:40:25,840
to buy virtual real estate.

760
00:40:25,841 --> 00:40:28,840
But the most significant difference about this game

761
00:40:28,841 --> 00:40:33,840
is its ability to teach players the value of ethical behavior.

762
00:40:33,841 --> 00:40:35,840
In a persistent virtual world,

763
00:40:35,841 --> 00:40:38,840
whatever you do is now instantly and forever

764
00:40:38,841 --> 00:40:41,840
part of the permanent history of that virtual world.

765
00:40:41,841 --> 00:40:45,840
And so therefore, the ramification of your deeds

766
00:40:45,841 --> 00:40:47,840
is much more important.

767
00:40:47,841 --> 00:40:51,840
You can't flippantly do something that you might otherwise

768
00:40:51,841 --> 00:40:54,840
consider unethical or poor behavior,

769
00:40:54,841 --> 00:40:56,840
because that deed, just like in the real world,

770
00:40:56,841 --> 00:40:59,840
will follow you continuously for the rest of your virtual life.

771
00:41:01,840 --> 00:41:04,840
Ultima and games like it have taken to heart

772
00:41:04,841 --> 00:41:07,840
that video games are a negative experience

773
00:41:07,841 --> 00:41:09,840
and turned it into a positive by making their games

774
00:41:09,841 --> 00:41:12,840
a kind of handbook of moral instruction.

775
00:41:12,841 --> 00:41:15,840
Like good literature, the interactive game experience

776
00:41:15,841 --> 00:41:18,840
now has the potential to educate and inspire

777
00:41:18,841 --> 00:41:20,840
as well as entertain.

778
00:41:26,840 --> 00:41:31,840
Blackthorn, you are indeed a fool to want, Britannia.

779
00:41:31,841 --> 00:41:35,340
once I had finished with it.

780
00:41:38,080 --> 00:41:41,780
Video games will never be as essential as food, clothing,

781
00:41:41,880 --> 00:41:45,620
or shelter, but they have become a multibillion-dollar industry

782
00:41:45,720 --> 00:41:49,120
by providing the space for adolescents and adults to dream,

783
00:41:49,220 --> 00:41:53,020
to fantasize, and to experience a kind of mastery

784
00:41:53,120 --> 00:41:55,760
not wholly available in the real world.

785
00:41:55,860 --> 00:41:59,360
And most of all, for all the genius of technology

786
00:41:59,361 --> 00:42:02,200
and sophistication of art and science,

787
00:42:02,300 --> 00:42:04,700
the end result is to tell a good story.

788
00:42:10,340 --> 00:42:12,840
So 10,000 years ago, people sat around a campfire

789
00:42:12,940 --> 00:42:14,700
and they listened to a story, and that seemed completely

790
00:42:14,800 --> 00:42:15,780
natural.

791
00:42:15,880 --> 00:42:18,340
And 30 years ago, they sat around the living room

792
00:42:18,440 --> 00:42:20,520
and they watched the television, and that also

793
00:42:20,620 --> 00:42:22,080
seemed completely natural.

794
00:42:22,180 --> 00:42:25,820
A time is coming where what will really seem natural to people

795
00:42:25,821 --> 00:42:28,620
is to sit down in front of a computer screen,

796
00:42:28,720 --> 00:42:31,000
whether it's in their living room or their den,

797
00:42:31,100 --> 00:42:34,300
or even at work, and be connected with other people

798
00:42:34,400 --> 00:42:37,800
around the world, sharing in a fantasy world experience,

799
00:42:37,900 --> 00:42:40,840
making their own story, being a hero all by themselves.

800
00:42:44,000 --> 00:42:45,340
Ah!

801
00:42:45,440 --> 00:42:46,840
I'm getting out of here.

802
00:42:46,940 --> 00:42:48,680
This world's for suckers.

